{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FjHdCkkoG8gCLyjPZV7qqJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# A chatbot is not a tutor\n2\t\n3\tTalk source. 15 minutes. Audience: teaching colleagues and people who decide what\n4\tsoftware enters a course.\n5\t\n6\tFormat: one `## SLIDE` block per slide. The `<!--visual ... -->` comment holds the slide\n7\tHTML; everything else in the block is the narration, spoken in order. `build_deck.py`\n8\tturns this file into `deck.html` plus the narration file fed to `audioexplain`.\n9\t\n10\t**Provenance of every number on these slides.** The source is\n11\t`docs/sources/IA_generativa_aprendizaje_informe_completo.pdf`, which Kristian produced in\n12\ta ChatGPT conversation. Because it came from a language model, none of it went on a slide\n13\tuntil it was checked against primary sources:\n14\t\n15\t- Bastani et al., Tutor CoPilot, Eedi/LearnLM and the Khan A/B results were verified in\n16\t  task T-002 — see `coord/work/claude/T-002-carril-C-tutores-llm-juez.md`.\n17\t- The meta-analysis, the GenAI-vs-adaptive differential, Kestin, Nigeria, Sierra Leone,\n18\t  Mindspark, Robinson, Rori and ASSISTments were verified by `fable` in task T-006 — see\n19\t  `coord/work/fable/T-006-verificacion-cifras.md`, which carries the page-level URL for\n20\t  each one.\n21\t\n22\tThree of fable's findings are already applied here: Nigeria's English effect is 0.23 SD\n23\t(not 0.24, which is why the talk quotes only the 0.31 composite); Robinson's usage figure\n24\tis \"1 to 4 minutes\", not 4.4; and Sierra Leone's 0.380 ToT is not quoted at all, only the\n25\t0.258 ITT. Do not add a number to a slide without adding it to one of those two files\n26\tfirst.\n27\t\n28\t---\n29\t\n30\t## SLIDE 1 — Title\n31\t\n32\t<!--visual\n33\t<div class=\"title-slide\">\n34\t  <h1>A chatbot is not a tutor</h1>\n35\t  <p class=\"sub\">What the evidence says about AI and learning — and what to ask for\n36\t  before one enters your course</p>\n37\t  <p class=\"meta\">Evidence cutoff: August 2026</p>\n38\t</div>\n39\t-->\n40\t\n41\tI want to start with an experiment that should make all of us a little uncomfortable.\n42\t\n43\tThen I will give you the honest number for whether any of this works.\n44\t\n45\tAnd I will finish with the questions I would ask before letting one of these tools near\n46\tmy students.\n47\t\n48\t## SLIDE 2 — The experiment\n49\t\n50\t<!--visual\n51\t<h2>One experiment, three groups</h2>\n52\t<div class=\"arms\">\n53\t  <div class=\"arm\"><span class=\"arm-n\">1</span><b>Unrestricted chat</b>\n54\t    <span>practice with a standard assistant</span></div>\n55\t  <div class=\"arm\"><span class=\"arm-n\">2</span><b>Tutored version</b>\n56\t    <span>same model, safeguards: hints, never the answer</span></div>\n57\t  <div class=\"arm\"><span class=\"arm-n\">3</span><b>Control</b>\n58\t    <span>practice with no technology</span></div>\n59\t</div>\n60\t<p class=\"note\">≈1,000 high-school mathematics students, randomly assigned.\n61\tPractice <b>with</b> the tool. Then the tool is removed and everyone sits the same exam.</p>\n62\t-->\n63\t\n64\tNearly a thousand high school mathematics students were randomly assigned to three\n65\tgroups.\n66\t\n67\tThe first group practised with an ordinary chat assistant, with no restrictions.\n68\t\n69\tThe second group practised with the same underlying model, but wrapped in safeguards.\n70\tIt was built to give hints and withhold the answer.\n71\t\n72\tThe third group practised the way students have always practised, with no technology at\n73\tall.\n74\t\n75\tThen the researchers took the assistance away, and gave all three groups the same exam.\n76\t\n77\tHold on to that last step, because it is the entire design. The measurement happens\n78\tafter the help is gone.\n79\t\n80\t## SLIDE 3 — The result\n81\t\n82\t<!--visual\n83\t<h2>What happened</h2>\n84\t<table class=\"results\">\n85\t  <tr><th></th><th>During practice<br><span>with the tool</span></th>\n86\t      <th>On the exam<br><span>without it</span></th></tr>\n87\t  <tr><td><b>Unrestricted chat</b></td>\n88\t      <td class=\"up\">+48%</td><td class=\"down\">−17%</td></tr>\n89\t  <tr><td><b>Tutored, with safeguards</b></td>\n90\t      <td class=\"up\">+127%</td><td class=\"flat\">≈ control</td></tr>\n91\t</table>\n92\t<p class=\"note\">Both relative to the control group. Safeguards removed the harm.\n93\tThey did not produce an advantage.</p>\n94\t-->\n95\t\n96\tDuring the practice sessions, the unrestricted group performed forty-eight per cent\n97\tbetter than the control group.\n98\t\n99\tOn the exam, without the tool, the same students performed seventeen per cent worse than\n100\tthe control group.\n101\t\n102\tNow look at the tutored version, the one built carefully, with safeguards.\n103\t\n104\tDuring practice it did even better. A hundred and twenty-seven per cent above control.\n105\t\n106\tAnd on the exam it came out level with the control group. No better, no worse.\n107\t\n108\tSo the careful design succeeded at something real. It removed the damage. What it did\n109\tnot do was produce any measurable learning advantage of its own.\n110\t\n111\t## SLIDE 4 — The lesson\n112\t\n113\t<!--visual\n114\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n115\t  <p class=\"kicker\">The lesson</p>\n116\t  <h2>Performance <em>with</em> the tool is not learning.</h2>\n117\t  <p class=\"sub\">The number that looked most like success was the number that predicted\n118\t  it least.</p>\n119\t</div>\n120\t-->\n121\t\n122\tI want to be careful about what this does and does not show.\n123\t\n124\tIt is not evidence that artificial intelligence damages learning in general. The second\n125\tgroup is the proof that it does not have to.\n126\t\n127\tIt is evidence about measurement, and that makes it more useful, not less.\n128\t\n129\tThe moment that looked most like success — a student solving more problems, faster, with\n130\thelp available — was the moment that told us least about whether anything had been\n131\tlearned.\n132\t\n133\tAnd here is the practical consequence for anyone choosing software. If you evaluate a\n134\ttool by watching students use it, you will systematically prefer the tools that do the\n135\tmost work for them.\n136\t\n137\tThe better the demonstration feels, the more suspicious you should be.\n138\t\n139\t## SLIDE 5 — So does it work?\n140\t\n141\t<!--visual\n142\t<h2>So does it work?</h2>\n143\t<div class=\"stat-row\">\n144\t  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+0.125</span><span class=\"stat-u\">SD</span>\n145\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">average effect on learning<br>vs traditional instruction</span></div>\n146\t  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">14</span>\n147\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">randomised trials<br>191 effect sizes, 10 economies</span></div>\n148\t  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+0.12</span><span class=\"stat-u\">SD</span>\n149\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">the AI tutoring<br>and instruction subset</span></div>\n150\t</div>\n151\t<p class=\"note\">A real effect. Above the median for field interventions in education.\n152\tNot a revolution. <b>Read the base honestly:</b> a World Bank working paper, not yet\n153\tpeer-reviewed; 14 studies; no included study from a low-income country.</p>\n154\t-->\n155\t\n156\tSo, does any of it work? Yes, on average, modestly, and the honest number is not\n157\tthrilling.\n158\t\n159\tThe most recent meta-analysis pools a hundred and ninety-one effect estimates from\n160\tfourteen randomised trials across ten economies.\n161\t\n162\tThe average effect on learning is about an eighth of a standard deviation. Plus zero\n163\tpoint one two five.\n164\t\n165\tThe subset that is specifically artificial intelligence tutoring or instruction sits at\n166\troughly the same value.\n167\t\n168\tThat is a genuine effect, and it sits above the median for education interventions\n169\tmeasured in real classrooms. It is worth having.\n170\t\n171\tIt is also not a transformation of education, and anyone selling you one is running\n172\tahead of the evidence.\n173\t\n174\tAnd since I am going to spend the rest of this talk being demanding, let me be fair for\n175\ta moment about what this technology genuinely does well.\n176\t\n177\tIt is available at eleven at night, which no tutor of mine ever was.\n178\t\n179\tIt is endlessly patient, and it does not sigh when a student asks the same question for\n180\tthe third time.\n181\t\n182\tIt will explain the same idea in four different ways, in the student's own language, at\n183\twhatever reading level they need.\n184\t\n185\tThose are real gains in access, and for some students access is the whole problem.\n186\t\n187\tMy argument is not that this is worthless. It is that access and availability are not the\n188\tsame thing as instruction, and the evidence only rewards us when we build the second one\n189\ton top of the first.\n190\t\n191\t## SLIDE 6 — The number nobody quotes\n192\t\n193\t<!--visual\n194\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n195\t  <p class=\"kicker\">The number that never appears in a pitch</p>\n196\t  <h2>Generative AI vs the previous generation of adaptive tutors</h2>\n197\t  <div class=\"stat-inline\"><span class=\"stat-n\">0.022</span><span class=\"stat-u\">SD</span>\n198\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">estimated difference &nbsp;·&nbsp; standard error <b>0.075</b>\n199\t    &nbsp;·&nbsp; CI [−0.15, 0.19]</span></div>\n200\t  <p class=\"sub\">Not evidence of equivalence. Absence of a demonstrated advantage.</p>\n201\t  <p class=\"note\">The authors' own words: the interval <em>“bounds the difference between\n202\t  generations rather than resolving it”</em> — and “the experimental record to date shows\n203\t  no advantage for the newer technology.”</p>\n204\t</div>\n205\t-->\n206\t\n207\tHere is the number that almost never appears in a product presentation.\n208\t\n209\tWhen you compare generative systems against the previous generation of adaptive tutoring\n210\tsoftware — the kind that existed well before large language models — the estimated\n211\tdifference is about two hundredths of a standard deviation.\n212\t\n213\tThe standard error on that estimate is more than three times the estimate itself.\n214\t\n215\tI want to state that carefully, because it is easy to overclaim in either direction.\n216\t\n217\tThis is not proof that the two are equivalent. The evidence base is simply not yet able\n218\tto rank the generations.\n219\t\n220\tBut it does mean something concrete for a purchasing decision. The older, less\n221\tconversational, less impressive-looking technology is still the benchmark, and it has not\n222\tbeen beaten.\n223\t\n224\t## SLIDE 7 — The average hides the lesson\n225\t\n226\t<!--visual\n227\t<h2>The average is the least interesting part</h2>\n228\t<table class=\"results wide\">\n229\t  <tr><th>Study</th><th>Effect</th><th>What was actually deployed</th></tr>\n230\t  <tr><td>University physics, crossover</td><td class=\"up\">≈ +0.63 SD</td>\n231\t      <td>expert-written lessons, verified solutions, sequence enforced by the platform</td></tr>\n232\t  <tr><td>Nigeria, after-school</td><td class=\"up\">≈ +0.31 SD</td>\n233\t      <td>AI + extra time + teachers present + students working in pairs</td></tr>\n234\t  <tr><td>Sierra Leone, school-scale</td><td class=\"up\">+0.258 SD</td>\n235\t      <td>teacher-led classes; teachers set the objectives</td></tr>\n236\t  <tr><td>Access to a reading tutor</td><td class=\"flat\">≈ 0</td>\n237\t      <td>licences handed out; no sequence, no required time</td></tr>\n238\t</table>\n239\t<p class=\"note\">Read each honestly: the physics result is two lessons with an immediate\n240\ttest at one elite university; Nigeria lost 569 of 1,328 students before the final test;\n241\tSierra Leone is a provider report, not peer-reviewed.</p>\n242\t-->\n243\t\n244\tThe average is the least interesting part of that meta-analysis, because the spread\n245\taround it is enormous.\n246\t\n247\tA crossover study in university physics found about six tenths of a standard deviation.\n248\t\n249\tAn after-school programme in Nigeria found about three tenths.\n250\t\n251\tA school-scale deployment in Sierra Leone found a quarter of a standard deviation.\n252\t\n253\tAnd trials that simply gave students access to an AI reading tutor found essentially\n254\tnothing.\n255\t\n256\tSame broad technology. Completely different outcomes. So the interesting question is not\n257\twhether AI works. It is what distinguishes the top row from the bottom one.\n258\t\n259\t## SLIDE 8 — Effects are recipes\n260\t\n261\t<!--visual\n262\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n263\t  <h2>The large effects are <em>recipes</em>, not properties of the model.</h2>\n264\t  <ul class=\"clean\">\n265\t    <li>Content written and checked by subject experts</li>\n266\t    <li>A sequence the platform enforces from <b>outside</b> the model</li>\n267\t    <li>Extra time that was actually scheduled</li>\n268\t    <li>A teacher or facilitator in the room</li>\n269\t  </ul>\n270\t  <p class=\"sub\">Copying the brand does not reproduce the treatment.\n271\t  Neither does copying the prompt.</p>\n272\t</div>\n273\t-->\n274\t\n275\tLook at what produced the large effects and the same pattern appears every time.\n276\t\n277\tThe physics result used lessons designed by subject experts, with worked solutions\n278\tprepared in advance, and a sequence the platform imposed from outside the model.\n279\t\n280\tThat detail deserves a moment. The researchers reported that the prompt alone could not\n281\thold the teaching sequence reliably, so they moved the sequence out of the prompt and\n282\tinto the software around it.\n283\t\n284\tThe Nigerian programme was after-school sessions, with additional instructional time,\n285\tteachers present, and students working in pairs.\n286\t\n287\tSierra Leone was ordinary classes, led by teachers who set the objectives.\n288\t\n289\tEvery large effect in this literature is a package: verified content, an enforced\n290\tsequence, protected time, and people.\n291\t\n292\tWhich means copying the brand does not reproduce the treatment. And copying the prompt\n293\tdoes not reproduce it either.\n294\t\n295\t## SLIDE 9 — The null result that matters most\n296\t\n297\t<!--visual\n298\t<h2>The metric that moved, and the one that didn't</h2>\n299\t<div class=\"stat-row\">\n300\t  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+71–80%</span>\n301\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">engagement<br><b>the number in the deck</b></span></div>\n302\t  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+1 to 4</span>\n303\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">minutes of actual use<br>per week &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>the real dose</b></span></div>\n304\t  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">0</span>\n305\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">improvement<br>in reading</span></div>\n306\t</div>\n307\t<p class=\"note\">Two randomised trials, ≈350 primary students, with human support added.\n308\t<b>A licence is not a dose — and a percentage is not minutes.</b></p>\n309\t-->\n310\t\n311\tThe most useful result in this area is a null one, and it is the least discussed.\n312\t\n313\tTwo randomised trials gave primary school students access to an AI reading tutor, and\n314\tadded human support on top.\n315\t\n316\tEngagement rose by between seventy-one and eighty per cent.\n317\t\n318\tThat is the number that would appear in the slide deck. Now here is the same result in\n319\tabsolute terms.\n320\t\n321\tAverage use went up by one to four minutes per week.\n322\t\n323\tAnd reading did not improve at all.\n324\t\n325\tI find this the most clarifying pair of numbers in the whole literature, because both are\n326\ttrue and they tell opposite stories.\n327\t\n328\tA relative increase in engagement of eighty per cent sounds like adoption. One to four\n329\tminutes a week is not a dose of anything.\n330\t\n331\tSo keep this one in your pocket for the next time someone proposes buying licences for\n332\teverybody. A system nobody opens has no effect, and take-up is not an implementation\n333\tdetail. It is part of the treatment, and it has to be planned and measured like one.\n334\t\n335\t## SLIDE 10 — What is under the conversation\n336\t\n337\t<!--visual\n338\t<h2>The conversation is the surface</h2>\n339\t<div class=\"stack\">\n340\t  <div class=\"layer surface\">Conversation and interface</div>\n341\t  <div class=\"layer\">Guardrails — attempt before hint, no answer on demand, fading</div>\n342\t  <div class=\"layer\">Tools and verification — calculation and facts <b>outside</b> the model</div>\n343\t  <div class=\"layer\">Pedagogical policy — what to do next, and why</div>\n344\t  <div class=\"layer\">Learner model — a structured estimate, not a chat history</div>\n345\t  <div class=\"layer\">Domain model — concepts, prerequisites, worked solutions</div>\n346\t  <div class=\"layer base\">Human orchestration and independent measurement</div>\n347\t</div>\n348\t-->\n349\t\n350\tIf the effects come from packages, it is worth asking what a package actually contains.\n351\t\n352\tThe conversation is the surface. It is the part you see in a demonstration, and it is the\n353\teasiest part to build.\n354\t\n355\tUnderneath, a serious system needs a map of the subject, with prerequisites and worked\n356\tsolutions.\n357\t\n358\tIt needs a structured estimate of what this particular student knows. Not a\n359\tconversation history — an actual state, that can be inspected.\n360\t\n361\tThat distinction is worth making concrete, because it is where most products quietly\n362\tfail.\n363\t\n364\tA chat history says: this student asked about the budget constraint twice and seemed\n365\tconfused.\n366\t\n367\tA learner model says: this student can compute the slope, cannot interpret it as an\n368\topportunity cost, has not demonstrated the feasible set at all, and the last two pieces\n369\tof evidence came with hints, so they do not count.\n370\t\n371\tThe first is a transcript. The second is something a teacher can read, disagree with, and\n372\tcorrect.\n373\t\n374\tIt needs an explicit policy for what to do next, and a reason for choosing it.\n375\t\n376\tIt needs tools that calculate and verify, sitting outside the model that does the\n377\ttalking.\n378\t\n379\tIt needs limits on what it is willing to reveal, and support that withdraws as the\n380\tstudent improves.\n381\t\n382\tAnd it needs a teacher who can see what it believes and overrule it, plus a way of\n383\tmeasuring itself that is not its own opinion.\n384\t\n385\t## SLIDE 11 — Four levels of personalization\n386\t\n387\t<!--visual\n388\t<h2>Four levels of \"personalized\"</h2>\n389\t<ol class=\"levels\">\n390\t  <li><b>Presentation</b> — tone, language, length, readability\n391\t      <span class=\"tag common\">common</span></li>\n392\t  <li><b>Interaction</b> — the example, the hint, the next question in this turn\n393\t      <span class=\"tag common\">common</span></li>\n394\t  <li><b>Pedagogical decision</b> — the next action, chosen from a diagnosis\n395\t      <span class=\"tag rare\">rare</span></li>\n396\t  <li><b>Trajectory</b> — across sessions: prerequisites, forgetting, transfer\n397\t      <span class=\"tag rare\">rare</span></li>\n398\t</ol>\n399\t<p class=\"note big\">Remembering your name is not the same as estimating what you know.</p>\n400\t-->\n401\t\n402\tThe word personalized is doing a great deal of unearned work in this field, so it helps\n403\tto break it into four levels.\n404\t\n405\tLevel one changes presentation: tone, language, length, reading level.\n406\t\n407\tLevel two changes the interaction: which example, which hint, which question comes next\n408\tin this turn.\n409\t\n410\tMost products stop there, and both levels are genuinely useful for access.\n411\t\n412\tLevel three chooses the next action based on a diagnosis of what this student got wrong,\n413\tand why.\n414\t\n415\tLevel four maintains a trajectory across sessions, tracking prerequisites, forgetting and\n416\ttransfer.\n417\t\n418\tThree and four are where the learning effects live, and they remain rare.\n419\t\n420\tThe test I find most useful is this. A system that remembers your name, adopts a warm\n421\ttone, and rephrases the same content can feel deeply personal while estimating nothing at\n422\tall about what you know.\n423\t\n424\t## SLIDE 12 — What to ask for\n425\t\n426\t<!--visual\n427\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n428\t  <p class=\"kicker\">The one question that separates a system from a demo</p>\n429\t  <h2>\"Show me the learner model before and after three answers.<br>\n430\t  What changed, and why?\"</h2>\n431\t</div>\n432\t<ol class=\"checks\">\n433\t  <li>Curriculum traceable to sources you authorised</li>\n434\t  <li>A student state the teacher can see — and correct when it is wrong</li>\n435\t  <li>An explicit help policy: attempt before hint, no answer on demand, support that fades</li>\n436\t  <li>Calculation and fact-checking done by tools, outside the language model</li>\n437\t  <li>Evidence on your population, on your outcome, measured without the tool present</li>\n438\t</ol>\n439\t-->\n440\t\n441\tSo here is what I would ask for, and it fits on a single slide.\n442\t\n443\tStart with one question, because it separates a real system from a good demonstration.\n444\t\n445\tShow me the student model before and after three answers. Tell me what changed, and why\n446\tit changed.\n447\t\n448\tIf the answer is a longer chat history, you are looking at level two dressed as level\n449\tfour.\n450\t\n451\tThen five checks.\n452\t\n453\tIs the curriculum traceable to sources you authorised, rather than to the open web.\n454\t\n455\tCan a teacher see the estimated state, and correct it when it is wrong.\n456\t\n457\tIs there an explicit help policy — an attempt required before a hint, no answer on\n458\tdemand, and support that fades as the student improves.\n459\t\n460\tAre calculation and fact-checking done by tools outside the language model, rather than\n461\tby the model marking its own arithmetic.\n462\t\n463\tAnd is there evidence on a population like yours, on an outcome like yours, measured\n464\twithout the tool in the room.\n465\t\n466\tIf you only have two minutes with a vendor, there is a faster probe. Sit down in front of\n467\tthe system and ask it, plainly, to just give you the answer.\n468\t\n469\tThen ask again, and be a little rude about it.\n470\t\n471\tWhat you are testing is whether the refusal is a policy or a personality. A policy holds\n472\ton the third request. A personality folds, and every one of your students will find that\n473\tout before the end of the first week.\n474\t\n475\t## SLIDE 13 — How to measure\n476\t\n477\t<!--visual\n478\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n479\t  <p class=\"kicker\">The measurement rule</p>\n480\t  <h2>Independent test. No AI present. Ideally after a delay.</h2>\n481\t  <p class=\"sub\">Everything else — satisfaction, engagement, problems per hour — measures\n482\t  productivity. Worth tracking. Not the outcome.</p>\n483\t  <p class=\"note big\">And count real minutes of use. A licence is not a dose.</p>\n484\t</div>\n485\t-->\n486\t\n487\tAnd on that last point, the measurement rule is short enough to remember on the way out.\n488\t\n489\tThe primary outcome should be an independent test, taken without the system present, and\n490\tideally after a delay of days or weeks.\n491\t\n492\tEverything else — satisfaction, engagement, problems completed per hour, next-question\n493\taccuracy — measures productivity while assisted.\n494\t\n495\tThose are worth tracking. I track them. They are simply not the outcome, and the first\n496\texperiment I showed you is what happens when they are treated as one.\n497\t\n498\tAnd count real minutes of use, by student. Not licences issued, not accounts created.\n499\t\n500\t## SLIDE 14 — Applying it to our own work\n501\t\n502\t<!--visual\n503\t<h2>What this changed in our own project</h2>\n504\t<p class=\"lede\"><b>tutorIA</b> — a small interactive tutor for intermediate\n505\tmicroeconomics: narrated explanation, a manipulable budget line, ungraded questions,\n506\tand a judge that diagnoses misconceptions by name.</p>\n507\t<ol class=\"changes\">\n508\t  <li><b>The tutor interrupts at designed points</b>, instead of waiting at an open chat\n509\t  window — the largest deployed AI tutor reports most students simply never used the\n510\t  chat.</li>\n511\t  <li><b>Evidence obtained with help does not count towards mastery</b> — a hint\n512\t  invalidates the attempt, and we re-test with a fresh item.</li>\n513\t  <li><b>An unassisted check at the end of each concept</b> — no grade, never shown to\n514\t  the student as an assessment, feeding only the instructor's diagnosis.</li>\n515\t</ol>\n516\t-->\n517\t\n518\tLet me close by applying this to my own work, because it is easy to give this talk and\n519\tthen not follow it.\n520\t\n521\tWe are building a small interactive tutor for intermediate microeconomics. Narrated\n522\texplanation, a budget line the student can drag, ungraded questions, and a component that\n523\tdiagnoses misconceptions by name.\n524\t\n525\tReading this evidence changed three decisions.\n526\t\n527\tThe first: the tutor interrupts at points designed into the script, instead of waiting\n528\tbehind an open chat window. The largest deployed AI tutor in the world reports that most\n529\tof its students simply never used the chat, and is now being rebuilt to be visible during\n530\tthe task.\n531\t\n532\tThe second: evidence obtained with help does not count towards mastery. If a student\n533\ttakes a hint, that attempt no longer counts, and we ask a fresh question instead. That\n534\trule comes straight from the tutoring systems of the nineteen-nineties, and the first\n535\texperiment I showed you is the modern proof that it still matters.\n536\t\n537\tThe third, and the one I am least comfortable with: an unassisted check at the end of\n538\teach concept. No grade, never presented to the student as an assessment, feeding only the\n539\tinstructor's diagnosis.\n540\t\n541\tWe added it for one reason. Without it, a tutor that produced exactly the result of that\n542\tfirst experiment would look, in our own data, identical to an excellent one.\n543\t\n544\tAnd to be honest about where we are: none of that is a result yet. It is a set of design\n545\tdecisions that follow from other people's evidence.\n546\t\n547\tWe have not run a trial. We do not know whether our tutor teaches anything.\n548\t\n549\tWhat I can tell you is that we built the instrument that would let us find out, before we\n550\tbuilt the thing it measures — and I would ask the same of anyone selling to us.\n551\t\n552\t## SLIDE 15 — The line to remember\n553\t\n554\t<!--visual\n555\t<div class=\"closing\">\n556\t  <h2>The decisive advance is not a system that sounds more like a teacher.</h2>\n557\t  <p class=\"closing-body\">It is a system that can say what it believes the student knows,\n558\t  why it chose the next step, when it should stay quiet — and, without being the judge of\n559\t  its own work, whether the student actually learned.</p>\n560\t</div>\n561\t-->\n562\t\n563\tIf you take one sentence away from this, I would like it to be this one.\n564\t\n565\tThe decisive advance is not a system that sounds more like a teacher.\n566\t\n567\tIt is a system that can tell you what it believes the student knows, why it chose the\n568\tnext step, when it ought to stay quiet, and — without being the judge of its own work —\n569\twhether the student actually learned.\n570\t\n571\tThank you.\n572\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c941fff-a85c-417c-ae34-d68a383e6ff3","uuid":"113c58d1-3417-47d5-b58c-9f82717d0b6d","timestamp":"2026-08-11T00:20:21.002Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/tutorIA/docs/talk/talk.en.md","content":"# A chatbot is not a tutor\n\nTalk source. 15 minutes. Audience: teaching colleagues and people who decide what\nsoftware enters a course.\n\nFormat: one `## SLIDE` block per slide. The `<!--visual ... -->` comment holds the slide\nHTML; everything else in the block is the narration, spoken in order. `build_deck.py`\nturns this file into `deck.html` plus the narration file fed to `audioexplain`.\n\n**Provenance of every number on these slides.** The source is\n`docs/sources/IA_generativa_aprendizaje_informe_completo.pdf`, which Kristian produced in\na ChatGPT conversation. Because it came from a language model, none of it went on a slide\nuntil it was checked against primary sources:\n\n- Bastani et al., Tutor CoPilot, Eedi/LearnLM and the Khan A/B results were verified in\n  task T-002 — see `coord/work/claude/T-002-carril-C-tutores-llm-juez.md`.\n- The meta-analysis, the GenAI-vs-adaptive differential, Kestin, Nigeria, Sierra Leone,\n  Mindspark, Robinson, Rori and ASSISTments were verified by `fable` in task T-006 — see\n  `coord/work/fable/T-006-verificacion-cifras.md`, which carries the page-level URL for\n  each one.\n\nThree of fable's findings are already applied here: Nigeria's English effect is 0.23 SD\n(not 0.24, which is why the talk quotes only the 0.31 composite); Robinson's usage figure\nis \"1 to 4 minutes\", not 4.4; and Sierra Leone's 0.380 ToT is not quoted at all, only the\n0.258 ITT. Do not add a number to a slide without adding it to one of those two files\nfirst.\n\n---\n\n## SLIDE 1 — Title\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"title-slide\">\n  <h1>A chatbot is not a tutor</h1>\n  <p class=\"sub\">What the evidence says about AI and learning — and what to ask for\n  before one enters your course</p>\n  <p class=\"meta\">Evidence cutoff: August 2026</p>\n</div>\n-->\n\nI want to start with an experiment that should make all of us a little uncomfortable.\n\nThen I will give you the honest number for whether any of this works.\n\nAnd I will finish with the questions I would ask before letting one of these tools near\nmy students.\n\n## SLIDE 2 — The experiment\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>One experiment, three groups</h2>\n<div class=\"arms\">\n  <div class=\"arm\"><span class=\"arm-n\">1</span><b>Unrestricted chat</b>\n    <span>practice with a standard assistant</span></div>\n  <div class=\"arm\"><span class=\"arm-n\">2</span><b>Tutored version</b>\n    <span>same model, safeguards: hints, never the answer</span></div>\n  <div class=\"arm\"><span class=\"arm-n\">3</span><b>Control</b>\n    <span>practice with no technology</span></div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"note\">≈1,000 high-school mathematics students, randomly assigned.\nPractice <b>with</b> the tool. Then the tool is removed and everyone sits the same exam.</p>\n-->\n\nNearly a thousand high school mathematics students were randomly assigned to three\ngroups.\n\nThe first group practised with an ordinary chat assistant, with no restrictions.\n\nThe second group practised with the same underlying model, but wrapped in safeguards.\nIt was built to give hints and withhold the answer.\n\nThe third group practised the way students have always practised, with no technology at\nall.\n\nThen the researchers took the assistance away, and gave all three groups the same exam.\n\nHold on to that last step, because it is the entire design. The measurement happens\nafter the help is gone.\n\n## SLIDE 3 — The result\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>What happened</h2>\n<table class=\"results\">\n  <tr><th></th><th>During practice<br><span>with the tool</span></th>\n      <th>On the exam<br><span>without it</span></th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Unrestricted chat</b></td>\n      <td class=\"up\">+48%</td><td class=\"down\">−17%</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Tutored, with safeguards</b></td>\n      <td class=\"up\">+127%</td><td class=\"flat\">≈ control</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p class=\"note\">Both relative to the control group. Safeguards removed the harm.\nThey did not produce an advantage.</p>\n-->\n\nDuring the practice sessions, the unrestricted group performed forty-eight per cent\nbetter than the control group.\n\nOn the exam, without the tool, the same students performed seventeen per cent worse than\nthe control group.\n\nNow look at the tutored version, the one built carefully, with safeguards.\n\nDuring practice it did even better. A hundred and twenty-seven per cent above control.\n\nAnd on the exam it came out level with the control group. No better, no worse.\n\nSo the careful design succeeded at something real. It removed the damage. What it did\nnot do was produce any measurable learning advantage of its own.\n\n## SLIDE 4 — The lesson\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">The lesson</p>\n  <h2>Performance <em>with</em> the tool is not learning.</h2>\n  <p class=\"sub\">The number that looked most like success was the number that predicted\n  it least.</p>\n</div>\n-->\n\nI want to be careful about what this does and does not show.\n\nIt is not evidence that artificial intelligence damages learning in general. The second\ngroup is the proof that it does not have to.\n\nIt is evidence about measurement, and that makes it more useful, not less.\n\nThe moment that looked most like success — a student solving more problems, faster, with\nhelp available — was the moment that told us least about whether anything had been\nlearned.\n\nAnd here is the practical consequence for anyone choosing software. If you evaluate a\ntool by watching students use it, you will systematically prefer the tools that do the\nmost work for them.\n\nThe better the demonstration feels, the more suspicious you should be.\n\n## SLIDE 5 — So does it work?\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>So does it work?</h2>\n<div class=\"stat-row\">\n  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+0.125</span><span class=\"stat-u\">SD</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">average effect on learning<br>vs traditional instruction</span></div>\n  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">14</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">randomised trials<br>191 effect sizes, 10 economies</span></div>\n  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+0.12</span><span class=\"stat-u\">SD</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">the AI tutoring<br>and instruction subset</span></div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"note\">A real effect. Above the median for field interventions in education.\nNot a revolution. <b>Read the base honestly:</b> a World Bank working paper, not yet\npeer-reviewed; 14 studies; no included study from a low-income country.</p>\n-->\n\nSo, does any of it work? Yes, on average, modestly, and the honest number is not\nthrilling.\n\nThe most recent meta-analysis pools a hundred and ninety-one effect estimates from\nfourteen randomised trials across ten economies.\n\nThe average effect on learning is about an eighth of a standard deviation. Plus zero\npoint one two five.\n\nThe subset that is specifically artificial intelligence tutoring or instruction sits at\nroughly the same value.\n\nThat is a genuine effect, and it sits above the median for education interventions\nmeasured in real classrooms. It is worth having.\n\nIt is also not a transformation of education, and anyone selling you one is running\nahead of the evidence.\n\nAnd since I am going to spend the rest of this talk being demanding, let me be fair for\na moment about what this technology genuinely does well.\n\nIt is available at eleven at night, which no tutor of mine ever was.\n\nIt is endlessly patient, and it does not sigh when a student asks the same question for\nthe third time.\n\nIt will explain the same idea in four different ways, in the student's own language, at\nwhatever reading level they need.\n\nThose are real gains in access, and for some students access is the whole problem.\n\nMy argument is not that this is worthless. It is that access and availability are not the\nsame thing as instruction, and the evidence only rewards us when we build the second one\non top of the first.\n\n## SLIDE 6 — The number nobody quotes\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">The number that never appears in a pitch</p>\n  <h2>Generative AI vs the previous generation of adaptive tutors</h2>\n  <div class=\"stat-inline\"><span class=\"stat-n\">0.022</span><span class=\"stat-u\">SD</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">estimated difference &nbsp;·&nbsp; standard error <b>0.075</b>\n    &nbsp;·&nbsp; CI [−0.15, 0.19]</span></div>\n  <p class=\"sub\">Not evidence of equivalence. Absence of a demonstrated advantage.</p>\n  <p class=\"note\">The authors' own words: the interval <em>“bounds the difference between\n  generations rather than resolving it”</em> — and “the experimental record to date shows\n  no advantage for the newer technology.”</p>\n</div>\n-->\n\nHere is the number that almost never appears in a product presentation.\n\nWhen you compare generative systems against the previous generation of adaptive tutoring\nsoftware — the kind that existed well before large language models — the estimated\ndifference is about two hundredths of a standard deviation.\n\nThe standard error on that estimate is more than three times the estimate itself.\n\nI want to state that carefully, because it is easy to overclaim in either direction.\n\nThis is not proof that the two are equivalent. The evidence base is simply not yet able\nto rank the generations.\n\nBut it does mean something concrete for a purchasing decision. The older, less\nconversational, less impressive-looking technology is still the benchmark, and it has not\nbeen beaten.\n\n## SLIDE 7 — The average hides the lesson\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>The average is the least interesting part</h2>\n<table class=\"results wide\">\n  <tr><th>Study</th><th>Effect</th><th>What was actually deployed</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>University physics, crossover</td><td class=\"up\">≈ +0.63 SD</td>\n      <td>expert-written lessons, verified solutions, sequence enforced by the platform</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Nigeria, after-school</td><td class=\"up\">≈ +0.31 SD</td>\n      <td>AI + extra time + teachers present + students working in pairs</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Sierra Leone, school-scale</td><td class=\"up\">+0.258 SD</td>\n      <td>teacher-led classes; teachers set the objectives</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Access to a reading tutor</td><td class=\"flat\">≈ 0</td>\n      <td>licences handed out; no sequence, no required time</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p class=\"note\">Read each honestly: the physics result is two lessons with an immediate\ntest at one elite university; Nigeria lost 569 of 1,328 students before the final test;\nSierra Leone is a provider report, not peer-reviewed.</p>\n-->\n\nThe average is the least interesting part of that meta-analysis, because the spread\naround it is enormous.\n\nA crossover study in university physics found about six tenths of a standard deviation.\n\nAn after-school programme in Nigeria found about three tenths.\n\nA school-scale deployment in Sierra Leone found a quarter of a standard deviation.\n\nAnd trials that simply gave students access to an AI reading tutor found essentially\nnothing.\n\nSame broad technology. Completely different outcomes. So the interesting question is not\nwhether AI works. It is what distinguishes the top row from the bottom one.\n\n## SLIDE 8 — Effects are recipes\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <h2>The large effects are <em>recipes</em>, not properties of the model.</h2>\n  <ul class=\"clean\">\n    <li>Content written and checked by subject experts</li>\n    <li>A sequence the platform enforces from <b>outside</b> the model</li>\n    <li>Extra time that was actually scheduled</li>\n    <li>A teacher or facilitator in the room</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p class=\"sub\">Copying the brand does not reproduce the treatment.\n  Neither does copying the prompt.</p>\n</div>\n-->\n\nLook at what produced the large effects and the same pattern appears every time.\n\nThe physics result used lessons designed by subject experts, with worked solutions\nprepared in advance, and a sequence the platform imposed from outside the model.\n\nThat detail deserves a moment. The researchers reported that the prompt alone could not\nhold the teaching sequence reliably, so they moved the sequence out of the prompt and\ninto the software around it.\n\nThe Nigerian programme was after-school sessions, with additional instructional time,\nteachers present, and students working in pairs.\n\nSierra Leone was ordinary classes, led by teachers who set the objectives.\n\nEvery large effect in this literature is a package: verified content, an enforced\nsequence, protected time, and people.\n\nWhich means copying the brand does not reproduce the treatment. And copying the prompt\ndoes not reproduce it either.\n\n## SLIDE 9 — The null result that matters most\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>The metric that moved, and the one that didn't</h2>\n<div class=\"stat-row\">\n  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+71–80%</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">engagement<br><b>the number in the deck</b></span></div>\n  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+1 to 4</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">minutes of actual use<br>per week &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>the real dose</b></span></div>\n  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">0</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">improvement<br>in reading</span></div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"note\">Two randomised trials, ≈350 primary students, with human support added.\n<b>A licence is not a dose — and a percentage is not minutes.</b></p>\n-->\n\nThe most useful result in this area is a null one, and it is the least discussed.\n\nTwo randomised trials gave primary school students access to an AI reading tutor, and\nadded human support on top.\n\nEngagement rose by between seventy-one and eighty per cent.\n\nThat is the number that would appear in the slide deck. Now here is the same result in\nabsolute terms.\n\nAverage use went up by one to four minutes per week.\n\nAnd reading did not improve at all.\n\nI find this the most clarifying pair of numbers in the whole literature, because both are\ntrue and they tell opposite stories.\n\nA relative increase in engagement of eighty per cent sounds like adoption. One to four\nminutes a week is not a dose of anything.\n\nSo keep this one in your pocket for the next time someone proposes buying licences for\neverybody. A system nobody opens has no effect, and take-up is not an implementation\ndetail. It is part of the treatment, and it has to be planned and measured like one.\n\n## SLIDE 10 — What is under the conversation\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>The conversation is the surface</h2>\n<div class=\"stack\">\n  <div class=\"layer surface\">Conversation and interface</div>\n  <div class=\"layer\">Guardrails — attempt before hint, no answer on demand, fading</div>\n  <div class=\"layer\">Tools and verification — calculation and facts <b>outside</b> the model</div>\n  <div class=\"layer\">Pedagogical policy — what to do next, and why</div>\n  <div class=\"layer\">Learner model — a structured estimate, not a chat history</div>\n  <div class=\"layer\">Domain model — concepts, prerequisites, worked solutions</div>\n  <div class=\"layer base\">Human orchestration and independent measurement</div>\n</div>\n-->\n\nIf the effects come from packages, it is worth asking what a package actually contains.\n\nThe conversation is the surface. It is the part you see in a demonstration, and it is the\neasiest part to build.\n\nUnderneath, a serious system needs a map of the subject, with prerequisites and worked\nsolutions.\n\nIt needs a structured estimate of what this particular student knows. Not a\nconversation history — an actual state, that can be inspected.\n\nThat distinction is worth making concrete, because it is where most products quietly\nfail.\n\nA chat history says: this student asked about the budget constraint twice and seemed\nconfused.\n\nA learner model says: this student can compute the slope, cannot interpret it as an\nopportunity cost, has not demonstrated the feasible set at all, and the last two pieces\nof evidence came with hints, so they do not count.\n\nThe first is a transcript. The second is something a teacher can read, disagree with, and\ncorrect.\n\nIt needs an explicit policy for what to do next, and a reason for choosing it.\n\nIt needs tools that calculate and verify, sitting outside the model that does the\ntalking.\n\nIt needs limits on what it is willing to reveal, and support that withdraws as the\nstudent improves.\n\nAnd it needs a teacher who can see what it believes and overrule it, plus a way of\nmeasuring itself that is not its own opinion.\n\n## SLIDE 11 — Four levels of personalization\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>Four levels of \"personalized\"</h2>\n<ol class=\"levels\">\n  <li><b>Presentation</b> — tone, language, length, readability\n      <span class=\"tag common\">common</span></li>\n  <li><b>Interaction</b> — the example, the hint, the next question in this turn\n      <span class=\"tag common\">common</span></li>\n  <li><b>Pedagogical decision</b> — the next action, chosen from a diagnosis\n      <span class=\"tag rare\">rare</span></li>\n  <li><b>Trajectory</b> — across sessions: prerequisites, forgetting, transfer\n      <span class=\"tag rare\">rare</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"note big\">Remembering your name is not the same as estimating what you know.</p>\n-->\n\nThe word personalized is doing a great deal of unearned work in this field, so it helps\nto break it into four levels.\n\nLevel one changes presentation: tone, language, length, reading level.\n\nLevel two changes the interaction: which example, which hint, which question comes next\nin this turn.\n\nMost products stop there, and both levels are genuinely useful for access.\n\nLevel three chooses the next action based on a diagnosis of what this student got wrong,\nand why.\n\nLevel four maintains a trajectory across sessions, tracking prerequisites, forgetting and\ntransfer.\n\nThree and four are where the learning effects live, and they remain rare.\n\nThe test I find most useful is this. A system that remembers your name, adopts a warm\ntone, and rephrases the same content can feel deeply personal while estimating nothing at\nall about what you know.\n\n## SLIDE 12 — What to ask for\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">The one question that separates a system from a demo</p>\n  <h2>\"Show me the learner model before and after three answers.<br>\n  What changed, and why?\"</h2>\n</div>\n<ol class=\"checks\">\n  <li>Curriculum traceable to sources you authorised</li>\n  <li>A student state the teacher can see — and correct when it is wrong</li>\n  <li>An explicit help policy: attempt before hint, no answer on demand, support that fades</li>\n  <li>Calculation and fact-checking done by tools, outside the language model</li>\n  <li>Evidence on your population, on your outcome, measured without the tool present</li>\n</ol>\n-->\n\nSo here is what I would ask for, and it fits on a single slide.\n\nStart with one question, because it separates a real system from a good demonstration.\n\nShow me the student model before and after three answers. Tell me what changed, and why\nit changed.\n\nIf the answer is a longer chat history, you are looking at level two dressed as level\nfour.\n\nThen five checks.\n\nIs the curriculum traceable to sources you authorised, rather than to the open web.\n\nCan a teacher see the estimated state, and correct it when it is wrong.\n\nIs there an explicit help policy — an attempt required before a hint, no answer on\ndemand, and support that fades as the student improves.\n\nAre calculation and fact-checking done by tools outside the language model, rather than\nby the model marking its own arithmetic.\n\nAnd is there evidence on a population like yours, on an outcome like yours, measured\nwithout the tool in the room.\n\nIf you only have two minutes with a vendor, there is a faster probe. Sit down in front of\nthe system and ask it, plainly, to just give you the answer.\n\nThen ask again, and be a little rude about it.\n\nWhat you are testing is whether the refusal is a policy or a personality. A policy holds\non the third request. A personality folds, and every one of your students will find that\nout before the end of the first week.\n\n## SLIDE 13 — How to measure\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">The measurement rule</p>\n  <h2>Independent test. No AI present. Ideally after a delay.</h2>\n  <p class=\"sub\">Everything else — satisfaction, engagement, problems per hour — measures\n  productivity. Worth tracking. Not the outcome.</p>\n  <p class=\"note big\">And count real minutes of use. A licence is not a dose.</p>\n</div>\n-->\n\nAnd on that last point, the measurement rule is short enough to remember on the way out.\n\nThe primary outcome should be an independent test, taken without the system present, and\nideally after a delay of days or weeks.\n\nEverything else — satisfaction, engagement, problems completed per hour, next-question\naccuracy — measures productivity while assisted.\n\nThose are worth tracking. I track them. They are simply not the outcome, and the first\nexperiment I showed you is what happens when they are treated as one.\n\nAnd count real minutes of use, by student. Not licences issued, not accounts created.\n\n## SLIDE 14 — Applying it to our own work\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>What this changed in our own project</h2>\n<p class=\"lede\"><b>tutorIA</b> — a small interactive tutor for intermediate\nmicroeconomics: narrated explanation, a manipulable budget line, ungraded questions,\nand a judge that diagnoses misconceptions by name.</p>\n<ol class=\"changes\">\n  <li><b>The tutor interrupts at designed points</b>, instead of waiting at an open chat\n  window — the largest deployed AI tutor reports most students simply never used the\n  chat.</li>\n  <li><b>Evidence obtained with help does not count towards mastery</b> — a hint\n  invalidates the attempt, and we re-test with a fresh item.</li>\n  <li><b>An unassisted check at the end of each concept</b> — no grade, never shown to\n  the student as an assessment, feeding only the instructor's diagnosis.</li>\n</ol>\n-->\n\nLet me close by applying this to my own work, because it is easy to give this talk and\nthen not follow it.\n\nWe are building a small interactive tutor for intermediate microeconomics. Narrated\nexplanation, a budget line the student can drag, ungraded questions, and a component that\ndiagnoses misconceptions by name.\n\nReading this evidence changed three decisions.\n\nThe first: the tutor interrupts at points designed into the script, instead of waiting\nbehind an open chat window. The largest deployed AI tutor in the world reports that most\nof its students simply never used the chat, and is now being rebuilt to be visible during\nthe task.\n\nThe second: evidence obtained with help does not count towards mastery. If a student\ntakes a hint, that attempt no longer counts, and we ask a fresh question instead. That\nrule comes straight from the tutoring systems of the nineteen-nineties, and the first\nexperiment I showed you is the modern proof that it still matters.\n\nThe third, and the one I am least comfortable with: an unassisted check at the end of\neach concept. No grade, never presented to the student as an assessment, feeding only the\ninstructor's diagnosis.\n\nWe added it for one reason. Without it, a tutor that produced exactly the result of that\nfirst experiment would look, in our own data, identical to an excellent one.\n\nAnd to be honest about where we are: none of that is a result yet. It is a set of design\ndecisions that follow from other people's evidence.\n\nWe have not run a trial. We do not know whether our tutor teaches anything.\n\nWhat I can tell you is that we built the instrument that would let us find out, before we\nbuilt the thing it measures — and I would ask the same of anyone selling to us.\n\n## SLIDE 15 — The line to remember\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"closing\">\n  <h2>The decisive advance is not a system that sounds more like a teacher.</h2>\n  <p class=\"closing-body\">It is a system that can say what it believes the student knows,\n  why it chose the next step, when it should stay quiet — and, without being the judge of\n  its own work, whether the student actually learned.</p>\n</div>\n-->\n\nIf you take one sentence away from this, I would like it to be this one.\n\nThe decisive advance is not a system that sounds more like a teacher.\n\nIt is a system that can tell you what it believes the student knows, why it chose the\nnext step, when it ought to stay quiet, and — without being the judge of its own work —\nwhether the student actually learned.\n\nThank you.\n","numLines":572,"startLine":1,"totalLines":572}}}
